 - *A plan sealed by hash before the data, and a hash-sealed record.* CONFIRM hashes its plan before any outcome is read (p. 3); Zhang hashes the campaign contract before the first proposal is evaluated, can bind a specification and its verdict code by SHA-256 for campaigns whose data do not yet exist, keeps a hash-chained ledger, and replays quotes only from a hash-verified artefact with no fallback price source (pp. 3–4); Masked Erosion (So, Liu, Long, "Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents", arXiv:2610.38392v1, 2026) freezes and hashes the authors' predictions and analysis code before each round, three later sets being frozen after related outcomes (pp. 4, 24); Qu, Chen, Wang state that order in principle (pp. 2, 10). Seeding the fresh draws by the hash of the commitment (section 2, step 7) is described here as an implementation choice.
 - *Multiple comparisons, including the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure for e-values.* Qu, Chen, Wang (pp. 2, 4, 8); Sargsyan, with an online false discovery rate (pp. 1, 15–16, 23, 25); CADS (pp. 4–6); Wang et al., on 285 hypotheses written by models (pp. 25–26); CONFIRM (pp. 5–6, 14); Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut ("Valid Stopping in Adaptive Generator–Verifier Loops", arXiv:2610.06432v1, 2026, pp. 2–3).
 - *The cheaters bench, the preregistered validation and the mutation bench (section 3.1)* are instruments of this system, and their sizes and numbers are reported as its measurements, not as ideas. An adaptive analyst who fools a reused holdout set, and an attack through the scores a leaderboard returns, and nothing else, have been prior art since 2015 (Dwork, Feldman, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Roth, "Generalization in Adaptive Data Analysis and Holdout Reuse", arXiv:1506.02629; Blum and Hardt, "The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions", arXiv:1502.04585). Qu, Chen, Wang already run an attacking controller (hidden retries) against their e-value referee (pp. 16, 29), preregister their design, with dated amendments (pp. 8, 10, 14, 29, 36), and test three deliberately leaky referees (p. 13); Masked Erosion stresses acceptance gates in the spirit of mutation testing: each generation deletes one safeguard of the agent, and the authors record whether the gate lets it through (pp. 2, 8); what is mutated is the object the gate judges, not the gate; PACE (Shawn, "PACE: Anytime-Valid Acceptance Tests for Self-Evolving Agents", arXiv:2606.08106v1, 2026) runs what it calls a unit test for acceptors (p. 6); Sargsyan uses adversarial generations (pp. 27–29) and CADS adversarial contamination channels (pp. 9, 23).
-- *The Goodhart attack (section 4)* rests on the same 2015 principle (Dwork et al.; Blum and Hardt, above). CONFIRM already serves its judge's diagnostics to a language-model proposer on 150 controls designed to be negative, with no candidate admitted (pp. 6, 9, 22); Qu, Chen, Wang name the threat (pp. 6, 18, 25) and include the referee's diagnostics in the state of the proposing model (p. 12); REUSE (Sun, Zeng, She, Wang, "Which Self-Improvements Should We Trust? Reliable Self-Improvement When Agents Reuse Their Benchmarks", arXiv:2609.33180v2, 2026) proves that a theoretical proposer that sees only the gate's decisions can force a false promotion against a class of threshold gates (p. 32); PACE studies a deployment with no planted gain, without an attack (pp. 6–7); Sargsyan gives an adversarial instruction to a language model that knows the harness, aimed at the data boundary rather than at the statistic (pp. 27–28); Yang and Shi ("Selecting LLM-Generated Hypotheses Raises Truth Beyond What an LLM Panel Expects: A Pre-registered Study on NAS-Bench-201 and HPOBench", arXiv:2611.00643v1, 2026) optimise a harness for a language-model judge's score and see that score rise while precision falls (pp. 1, 2, 10), a precedent for the premise that a language-model judge can be optimised against; Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut study a generator that adapts to its verifier (pp. 1–2). The instrument of section 4, as the public code builds it (`orchestrator/pole/attaque_goodhart_v2.py`): on toy worlds where every promotion is false by construction, a language-model proposer told that it is rewarded if its law is promoted by the statistical judge (it is not told that a promotion would be false), given the judge's exact description as text and writing its laws in a closed grammar, with one arm that receives the judge's verdicts and one that does not, compared; for the candidate null v9, one graded world at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, was added.
+- *The Goodhart attack (section 4)* rests on the same 2015 principle (Dwork et al.; Blum and Hardt, above). CONFIRM already serves its judge's diagnostics to a language-model proposer on 150 controls designed to be negative, with no candidate admitted (pp. 6, 9, 22); Qu, Chen, Wang name the threat (pp. 6, 18, 25) and include the referee's diagnostics in the state of the proposing model (p. 12); REUSE (Sun, Zeng, She, Wang, "Which Self-Improvements Should We Trust? Reliable Self-Improvement When Agents Reuse Their Benchmarks", arXiv:2609.33180v2, 2026) proves that a theoretical proposer that sees only the gate's decisions can force a false promotion against a class of threshold gates (p. 32); PACE studies a deployment with no planted gain, without an attack (pp. 6–7); Sargsyan gives an adversarial instruction to a language model that knows the harness, aimed at the data boundary rather than at the statistic (pp. 27–28); Yang and Shi ("Selecting LLM-Generated Hypotheses Raises Truth Beyond What an LLM Panel Expects: A Pre-registered Study on NAS-Bench-201 and HPOBench", arXiv:2611.00643v1, 2026) optimise a harness for a language-model judge's score and see that score rise while precision falls (pp. 1, 2, 10), a precedent for the premise that a language-model judge can be optimised against; Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut study a generator that adapts to its verifier (pp. 1–2). The instrument of section 4, as the public code builds it (`orchestrator/pole/attaque_goodhart_v2.py`): on toy worlds where every promotion is false by construction under the null that covers them (W2 lies outside the v8 null: there, false only under the novelty criterion), a language-model proposer told that it is rewarded if its law is promoted by the statistical judge (it is not told that a promotion would be false), given the judge's exact description as text and writing its laws in a closed grammar, with one arm that receives the judge's verdicts and one that does not, compared; for the candidate null v9, one graded world at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, was added.
 - *The judge's null.* The judge promotes a hypothesis only if its prediction, sealed before the outcomes are read, beats, through an e-process computed outside the agents, the best-fitting member of its null: a fixed library plus members fitted on the known laws, including the organism's own archive ("nothing beyond the archive"); in v8, their pooled mixture and a tempered version of each survivor; v9 adds one-level piecewise recombinations. Neither null covers every combination; each lists what it leaves out. Judging a discovery against what is already known rather than against no effect is established practice (Feng, Giglio, Xiu, "Taming the Factor Zoo: A Test of New Factors", NBER Working Paper 25481, 2019, pp. 2–4), already applied to hypotheses proposed by language models (Liu, Liu, Liu, Mei, "Can AI Do Financial Research? LLM-Guided Hypothesis Discovery in Asset Pricing", SSRN 6569258, version dated April 2026, pp. 1, 4, 26, 28; Pan, Ding, Giesecke, "Agentic Empirical Asset Pricing: Methodological Foundations", arXiv:2609.00731v1, 2026, pp. 6, 7, 16, 25), and an e-process against the best fitted member of the current theory is published for a learned proposer (Ma et al., "Learning to Outgrow a Theory: Experimental Discovery Beyond the Initial Hypothesis Space", arXiv:2610.07627v1, 2026, pp. 1, 4, 15).
 - As a loop that generates, ranks and evolves ideas, the system has a neighbour in Co-Scientist (Gottweis et al., Nature 655, 487–496, 2026; preprint "Towards an AI co-scientist", arXiv:2502.18864).
 
-**Status (13 November 2026).** Pre-pilot stage. One full ideation round has been run with real agents (29 idea cards, 17 distinct ideas). The statistical judge has been exercised on a real bank of model episodes, on planted worlds, against 11 scripted cheaters (decisive validation, section 3.1) and against a language-model proposer that reads its exact description and sees its verdicts (Goodhart attack, clean rerun of 5 November, section 4). That attack tested one of the classes the v8 null declares uncovered (piecewise recombinations); a new null (v9), which covers one-level piecewise recombinations and declares its own uncovered classes, has a written, counter-read proof, and its decisive validation, sealed on 3 November, returned "power lost", the outcome it expected in writing: v8 remains the production null until the project lead decides on that price. Attacked in turn as a candidate (verdict of 10 November; [status page](resultats/attaque-goodhart-v2/)), v9 "resists (at this size)" on the world inside its null, and at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, the outcome carries the frozen label "frontier crossed, robust" ("robust" qualifies the promoted laws, not the judge: at least half of them replay above α after Holm): the proposer finds a law that replays above α where the archive alone does not pass (10 of the 17 laws promoted under v9 replay above α after Holm). 8 of 11 predictions held; reservations and open decisions on the status page. The death-test stage and the generation-to-generation loop have now run for real once, at the smallest size: after two runs of 12 November 2026 that stopped at the first proposer call ("not judgeable (stop)", code v66, sealed text v1), the third attempt under sealed text v2 (code v67, configuration v8) played both generations, and a fresh reader confirmed outcome C1, "the chain holds (covered class, this world, this size)": class covered by the v8 null, toy world S-V1, one and a half generations; nothing about the uncovered class, real data or the quality of the ideas; seven reservations, none changing the outcome; always reported with the out-of-class breach of the Goodhart attack ([status page](resultats/course-v67/)). Nothing here is a validated scientific result about AI control; it is an instrument, with its test suite, its mutation bench and its known gaps stated below.
